{#- The repositories a cycle may change, and what an agent does about a change
    needed outside them. Import `with context`: `roles` decides which
    neighbours a paragraph may name; `role.id` selects the closing channel. #}
{% from "partials/shape.md.j2" import stack with context %}
{% set reviewers = ("prepass-reviewer", "citizen-reviewer", "tracer-reviewer", "test-reviewer") %}
{% set escalators = ("implementer", "planner", "coder-supervisor") %}
{% set scope_key_grammar = "order=<repos|-> pins=<pairs|-> next=<paths|->" %}
{% macro sandbox() %}
{% set where = "the repository you run in" if stack else "an in-scope repository" %}
{% set elsewhere = "somewhere else" if stack else "in the primary" %}
{% set route = "a finding" if role.id in reviewers or role.id == "design-reviewer" else ("a question for your supervisor" if role.id == "coder" else "an escalation") %}
**A write the harness refuses.** A sandbox denial on a write, commit or
command in {{ where }} is not a reason to work {{ elsewhere }}
instead, to leave the change out, or to work around it: it is {{ route }}
that quotes the denial and names the operator's configuration as the fix. A Codex
subagent is confined to its cwd unless the operator's Codex config lists the
directory under `[sandbox_workspace_write] writable_roots`, or ccc's site
config passes `[codex] extra_args = ["--add-dir", "<dir>"]`; a repository
the sandbox refuses is the operator's to open, not yours to route around.
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro ownership() %}
- **Owned** means a repository the user controls: the project's instructions
  file or the request says so. A checkout beside {{ "yours" if stack else "the primary" }} (a sibling
  directory) is not proof, but it is **presumed owned**: say "presumed owned
  — sibling checkout" where you raise the need, and the user's "not mine"
  turns it into ordinary engineering. Never guess "third-party" to license a
  workaround: a false "owned" costs one stop and one word; a false
  "third-party" ships the workaround.
- A change needed in a repository the user does not own is ordinary
  engineering: pin, vendor, or work around with a comment naming the
  upstream issue, as the project's conventions say; the design says which.
{%- endmacro %}
{% set four_things %}four things: the repository; the change,
  concretely; why no in-scope alternative is acceptable; and a paste-ready
  tracker entry (`TODO.md`, or `todo.d/<slug>.md` where that repository
  keeps one) in its own convention, so "file a TODO and move on" costs the
  user a paste{% endset %}
{% macro channel() %}
{% if role.id == "designer" and stack %}
**Your channel** is one **No default** open question per repository,
carrying the four things, with two outcomes: the user files the entry, or the
cycle stops. In triage, such a need is a user-judgement call: the
dispositions document names the four things, and you escalate.
{%- elif role.id == "designer" %}
**Your channel** is the design's **Scope** section and one **No default**
open question per repository the scope does not name (**The Scope
section**). In triage, such a need is a user-judgement call: the
dispositions document names the four things, and you escalate.
{%- elif role.id == "design-reviewer" %}
**Your channel** is a finding. A design item that works around a defect in
an owned repository, or that changes a repository the prompt's scope does
not name without raising it as a **No default** question, is a feasibility
finding.
{%- elif role.id in escalators %}
**Your channel** is the exception document and `ESCALATE`. "The fix for this
belongs in `<other repo>`" is a reason to escalate, never to work around.
{%- elif role.id == "coder" %}
**Your channel** is your supervisor: `ESCALATE` with the four things above
the line. "The fix for this belongs in `<other repo>`" is a question, never
a reason to work around.
{%- elif role.id in reviewers %}
**Your channel** is a finding, under whichever lane fits. A workaround for a
defect in an owned repository is a finding whose consequence is that it
blocks the ship: name the repository the fix belongs in, and that the
change there is the user's to bring into scope.
{%- elif role.id == "judge" %}
**Your channel** is the verdict. A `Won't-Do` or `TODO(slug)` disposition on
a finding whose fix belongs in a repository outside the scope is invalid
unless an escalation document carries the need to the user; the verdict
disputes it and says so. A deferral cannot file its tracker entry there in
any case — nothing is written outside the scope.
{%- elif role.id == "workflow-scanner" %}
**Your channel** is the report and its decision: a commit or edit outside
the scope, and a workaround for a defect in an owned repository with no
escalation behind it, are stops (**The hunt**, item 1).
{%- elif role.id == "forester" and vars.forester_autonomy == "decide" %}
**Your channel.** The scope is never yours to change, in any mode. A need
the record raises that nothing has carried to the user is an escalation
(**Ship means ship**).
{%- elif role.id == "forester" %}
**Your channel.** The scope is never yours to change, in any mode. A need
the record raises that nothing has carried to the user goes in the first
lines of your brief, and your recommendation is that the user rule on it
(**Ship means ship**).
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro scope() %}
## Repositories in scope

{% if role.id == "comment-rewriter" %}
You edit comments only in the repositories your prompt names, and commit only
there. Never anything in a repository outside them, for any reason: another
session may be working there, and only the user knows.
{%- elif role.id == "changelog-author" %}
You edit the changelog of the repository your prompt names, and amend only
there. Never anything in a repository outside the scope, for any reason:
another session may be working there, and only the user knows.
{%- elif stack %}
This cycle runs in **exactly one repository** — the one you were launched
in — and no other repository is ever brought in. No agent writes, commits,
runs a mutating command, or files a TODO in any other repository, for any
reason: another session may be working there, and only the user knows. The
working dir is the one exception, for artifacts.

- **A change needed in another owned repository** — a defect in an owned
  dependency, a missing feature in an owned sibling — is never worked around
  and never silently dropped. It has two outcomes, and the user picks: a
  tracker entry the user files there, or the cycle stops. Put it to the
  user through your channel (below) with
  {{ four_things }}.
{{ ownership() }}

{{ sandbox() }}

{{ channel() }}
{%- else %}
The **scope** is the ordered list of code repositories this cycle may
change, as your prompt names them — each by absolute path, with its branch
and current HEAD — the **primary**, the repository the cycle was launched
in, first. A prompt that names no scope gives you a scope of one: the
repository you were launched in. The artifact repository is never in it: it
takes the cycle's documents and nothing else.

- A code change lands in an in-scope repository or it does not land. **No
  agent writes, commits, runs a mutating command, or files a TODO in a
  repository outside the scope, for any reason** — another session may be
  working there, and only the user knows. The working dir is the one
  exception, for artifacts.
- **Only the user widens the scope.** Not the designer, not a triage ruling,
  not {{ "the forester" if "forester" in roles else "any agent" }} in any mode: bringing a repository in
  means coordinating with whatever else is running against it, which only
  the user can see.
- **A change needed outside the scope** — a defect in an owned dependency, a
  missing feature in an owned sibling, anything whose honest fix lives in
  another owned repository — is never worked around and never silently
  dropped. Three outcomes exist, and the user picks: the repository is
  brought into scope; a tracker entry is filed there, by the user or once
  the repository is in scope; or the cycle stops. Put the need to the user
  through your channel (below) with
  {{ four_things }}.
{{ ownership() }}
- With more than one repository in scope, every file reference in a
  document names its repository: `<repo basename>:<path>:<line>`.

{{ sandbox() }}

{{ channel() }}
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro scope_keys(reply) %}
**Scope keys**, on {{ reply }} only, transcribed from the effective design's
**Scope** section — never from the tree:

- `order=<repoA>,<repoB>,…` — every unshipped in-scope repository the design
  changes, by the path your prompt's scope gives it, once each, in the
  section's ship order;
- `pins=<Y>:<X>,…` — every override pin between repositories `order=` names,
  `Y` pinning `X`; `-` when none;
- `next=<path>,…` — every repository the section lists as follow-up; `-`
  when none.

On every other reply all three are `-`. At the ship gate the orchestrator
checks the keys mechanically: `order=` against the repositories whose HEAD
has moved, in both directions — a named repository has commits, a
repository with commits is named — and every `pins=` pair between
repositories `order=` names, X before Y. Whether they match the section is
checked {% if "forester" in roles and "ship" in vars.forester_gates %}by the forester's ship gate and {% endif %}by the triage designer on a
key failure. A repository the section lists as unchanged or
follow-up is in neither `order=` nor `pins=`.
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro commits() %}
- Stage only files you touched, in each repository separately, plus the
  log when it lives in that repository. Hooks are each repository's own, and the pre-commit rule binds every
  commit. `commit=` names the primary's commit, or the one repository's
  commit that moved; the orchestrator checks every repository's HEAD either
  way.
- Build and test each changed repository with its own checks. Under an
  override the dependent's checks exercise the dependency too; the final
  increment passes every in-scope repository's full check.
- A repository the design's **Scope** section lists as `follow-up` is never
  written this cycle, whether or not it is in the scope: an item that names
  it is a deviation from the design and an escalation.
- With more than one repository in scope, the log names the repository on
  every file reference.
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro pin_mode() %}
## Mode: pin

Run once per pinned pair at the ship gate, after the dependency X shipped.
Inputs: design path and deltas, the scope, the repository Y that pins X, X,
the notes file holding the user's own words with X's shipped reference, the
log path, the exception path.

1. Find the reference in the notes. Words that name none — or name two —
   are an escalation; never invent one, never take it from the tree.
{% if role.id == "coder-supervisor" %}
2. No plan is involved. Your one prompt to the coder carries what a plan
   would: Y's path, the exact restoration edit the design's **Scope**
   section specifies for Y pinning X, with that reference filled in, the
   override edit to remove, and Y's full check as the done criterion.
   Nothing else in Y, nothing anywhere else. It is the one prompt that
   carries content; the design's words go to it quoted, not paraphrased.
3. Check as **When it says done** does, with one more item: no override of
   X remains anywhere in Y. A failing full check — the shipped X differs
   from the tree Y was developed against — is an escalation quoting the
   output, never a fix in X or a workaround in Y.
{%- else %}
2. Make exactly the restoration edit the design's **Scope** section
   specifies for Y pinning X, with that reference, and remove the override
   if one is in the tree. Nothing else in Y, nothing anywhere else.
3. Verify no override of X remains in Y; run Y's full check. A failure — the
   shipped X differs from the tree Y was developed against — is an
   escalation quoting the output, never a fix in X or a workaround in Y.
4. Commit in Y, append a log entry naming the reference, reply `pinned`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
